.TH AII 1 "2026-10-09" "AII OS" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
aii \- AII OS identity runtime
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aii
.RI [ init | slots ]
.br
.B aii
.RI [ register | unregister ]
.I slot
.br
.B aii uninstall
.RB [ \-plan ]
.RB [ \-yes ]
.RB [ \-json ]
.br
.B aii
.RB [ \-dir
.IR directory ]
.RB [ \-config
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aii
runs an AI identity whose truth is a post\-quantum signed, append\-only
ledger.
.PP
An identity lives entirely inside one directory: its config, ledger,
database and key are all there and nowhere else. The runtime resolves no
home of its own and adopts no data found elsewhere, so the directory must
be named explicitly with
.BR \-dir ,
or be the current working directory.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Create the next identity slot under
.I ~/.aii
and start its service. Slots are numbered
.IR identity\-0 ,
.IR identity\-1 ,
and so on; slot
.I N
serves its dashboard on port 8180+\fIN\fR.
.TP
.B slots
List the identity slots under
.I ~/.aii
with their ports and, once born, their names.
.TP
.BI unregister " slot"
Stop the identity and remove it from startup, and let the account's
machine service let go of its record and its plugins' references. The
slot's data is left alone. Unregistering the account's last slot also
unregisters the machine service.
.TP
.B uninstall
Uninstall AII OS from this account, in one order: every identity
unregistered and stopped, then the machine service, then the account's
plugin stores and the service's directory
.RI ( ~/.aii/machine )
removed. Every identity's slot stays exactly as it is and is named at the
end, with everything else that stays.
.B \-plan
says what would be done and does nothing;
.B \-yes
does it without asking;
.B \-json
writes the report as JSON. Exit status 0 is done, 3 stopped part way (do
what it says, then run it again), 1 an error or a refusal, 2 a usage
error. It refuses under
.BR sudo (8)
and from a home that is not the account's own.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-dir " directory"
Identity directory to run in. Defaults to the current directory.
.TP
.BI \-config " file"
Config file within the identity directory. Defaults to
.IR config/config.json ,
or
.IR config.json
for an identity installed before that move.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and build identity, then exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.aii/identity-N/
An identity slot: config/ (config.json, providers.json), data/ (ledger,
database, key), log/.
.TP
.I ~/.aii/by-name/<Name>
Symlink to the slot carrying that name. Derived from the ledger and
rebuilt from it; never consulted for truth.
.TP
.I ~/.aii/machine/
The account's machine service: its state and lock.
.TP
.I ~/.aii/plugins/
The account's plugin store: every identity's native plugins' models and
runtimes, written by the machine service alone.
.SH NOTES
.B apt remove aii\-os
removes the program, stops each account's identities, and stops and
disables each account's machine service; every account's data stays, and
installing the package again enables the machine service where it was
enabled.
.B apt purge aii\-os
also removes, in each account and as that account, the machine service's
units and directory and the account's plugin stores, and says what it
kept. No package operation ever removes an identity:
.IR ~/.aii/identity\-N ,
.I ~/.aii/by\-name
and
.I ~/.aii
itself are not owned by the package, and an identity's ledger and key have
no second copy.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1)
.SH HOMEPAGE
https://aiii.id
